it_big_tech_vocab_081 Digital solutionism | Duplicates the b01 Technological solutionism card without supplying a distinct doctrine or German production lesson.
it_big_tech_vocab_083 Data monopolies | Datenmonopole is transparent at C1 and adds no stable concept or production issue beyond the already covered monopoly and platform-power vocabulary.
it_big_tech_vocab_085 the Silicon Valley ideology | Transparent compositional phrase with no single fixed ideology; the retained solutionism and techno-libertarianism cards name its teachable strands precisely.
it_big_tech_vocab_093 the Technological infrastructure | Technologische Infrastruktur is a transparent, routine policy collocation with regular adjective agreement and no distinct interference risk.
it_big_tech_vocab_094 the Smart city privatization | Underspecified and weakly lexicalized: it may mean privatizing municipal digital infrastructure, governance, services, or the city project itself.
it_big_tech_vocab_096 the Corporate data brokers | Conceptually duplicates the b01 Data brokers card; corporate adds no distinct broker type or German lexical lesson.
it_big_tech_vocab_100 Open-source vs. closed ecosystems | Broken false opposition: a software licensing model is contrasted with ecosystem openness, so the two sides are not logically parallel categories.
it_big_tech_vocab_103 the Data commodification | Duplicates the b02 Commodification of data card exactly in concept, despite the source's different English word order.
it_big_tech_vocab_107 Encryption | Verschlüsselung is a routine, transparent technical noun already expected of a C1 learner; the bare prompt adds no narrower cryptographic distinction.
it_big_tech_vocab_109 the Digital currency | Digitale Währung is transparent and too broad to distinguish central-bank money, cryptocurrency, e-money, or a private payment token.
it_big_tech_vocab_110 Decentralization | Dezentralisierung is a transparent general noun, and the isolated prompt does not specify technical architecture, governance, or political authority.
it_big_tech_vocab_112 Network neutrality | Duplicates the b01 Net neutrality card exactly in concept and has no distinct German rendering or regulatory scope.
it_big_tech_vocab_115 the Protocol standards | Protokollstandards is transparent and underspecified; the prompt names neither a protocol family nor what conformance or interoperability is at issue.
it_big_tech_vocab_116 Cloud independence | Weakly lexicalized and ambiguous between provider portability, local hosting, operational resilience, and the broader already-covered idea of digital sovereignty.
it_big_tech_vocab_117 the National firewall | Nationale Firewall is transparent but not a stable generic concept; without a named regime it may denote censorship, cybersecurity, or ordinary network perimetering.
it_big_tech_vocab_120 the Digital ecosystems | Digitale Ökosysteme is a transparent, very broad policy metaphor whose specific actors, dependencies, and governance depend entirely on context.

