it_big_tech_vocab_002 the Internet centrism => l’internet-centrisme | alts: []
R Specialist critical neologism, associated especially with analyses that treat an abstract internet as a single force with its own inherent logic.
T This is not political centrism practiced online; it names the habit of putting an abstract idea of the internet at the center of every explanation.
X L’internet-centrisme de certains réformateurs suppose que la connectivité démocratise mécaniquement la société, alors que les mêmes réseaux peuvent renforcer le contrôle autoritaire.
E The internet-centrism of certain reformers assumes that connectivity automatically democratizes society, whereas the same networks can reinforce authoritarian control.
C ['L’internet-centrisme']
N Materially repairs old_back: the French neologism is hyphenated and written as a single lexical unit, without internal capitalization or spacing. Self-review replaced an abstract possessor with the natural agentive réformateurs.
it_big_tech_vocab_004 Surveillance capitalism => le capitalisme de surveillance | alts: []
R Established critical term in political economy and technology studies for a business logic based on extracting and monetizing behavioral data.
T It does not merely denote a market selling surveillance equipment; surveillance describes the system’s method of accumulating and predicting behavior.
X Le capitalisme de surveillance transforme chaque interaction en matière première comportementale, sans accorder aux usagers de contrôle effectif sur les usages ultérieurs de leurs données.
E Surveillance capitalism turns every interaction into behavioral raw material without granting users effective control over the subsequent uses of their data.
C ['Le capitalisme de surveillance']
N Old_back gives the established French term but unnecessarily capitalizes its article.
it_big_tech_vocab_005 the Algorithmic bias => le biais algorithmique | alts: ['le biais des algorithmes']
R Neutral-formal term in research, algorithmic auditing and technology policy; the alternative is more explanatory and less terminological.
T Biais may arise from data, design or deployment context; préjugé algorithmique is too anthropomorphic and suggests an intentionally held opinion.
X Un biais algorithmique peut défavoriser systématiquement les candidates, même lorsque le modèle n’utilise pas le sexe comme variable explicite de décision.
E Algorithmic bias can systematically disadvantage female candidates even when the model does not use sex as an explicit decision variable.
C ['biais algorithmique']
N Old_back is accurate; the example prevents the mistaken inference that only an explicitly protected variable can generate bias.
it_big_tech_vocab_006 the Net neutrality => la neutralité du Net | alts: ['la neutralité de l’internet']
R Established telecommunications-policy term in France; the Net form remains conventional in institutional and journalistic writing.
T The term does not claim that online content is politically neutral; it concerns nondiscriminatory treatment of traffic by network access providers.
X La neutralité du Net interdit à un fournisseur de ralentir un service rival ou de facturer aux plateformes un accès prioritaire à ses abonnés.
E Net neutrality prohibits a provider from slowing a rival service or charging platforms for priority access to its subscribers.
C ['La neutralité du Net']
N Old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary initial capitalization; the example anchors the precise regulatory sense. Self-review clarified who charges whom for priority access.
it_big_tech_vocab_007 the Data brokers => les courtiers en données | alts: ['les courtiers de données']
R Established data-protection and technology-policy term; the en construction is especially natural in France-French institutional prose.
T A courtier en données is not any neutral data intermediary: the label usually foregrounds collecting, combining and selling personal profiles.
X Les courtiers en données combinent des historiques d’achat, des données de localisation et des centres d’intérêt pour vendre des profils rarement consultables ou rectifiables par les personnes concernées.
E Data brokers combine purchase histories, location data, and interests to sell profiles that the people concerned can rarely inspect or correct.
C ['Les courtiers en données']
N Old_back is accurate. Self-review retained the established plural because the source denotes the industry’s actors, then made the types of brokered data and the access problem more precise.
it_big_tech_vocab_009 the Filter bubble => la bulle de filtres | alts: []
R Established media-criticism metaphor for informational isolation attributed specifically to personalized algorithmic selection.
T It is not identical to a chambre d’écho: the bubble emphasizes personalized filtering, while the chamber emphasizes mutual reinforcement within a group.
X Imputer toute la polarisation à la bulle de filtres occulte les conflits sociaux et les campagnes organisées qui nourrissent également les clivages politiques.
E Attributing all polarization to the filter bubble obscures the social conflicts and organized campaigns that also fuel political divisions.
C ['la bulle de filtres']
N The exact-duplicate report assigns this item to BIG_TECH_VOCAB. Materially improves old_back: the established term uses plural filtres. Self-review replaced divisions publiques with the idiomatic clivages politiques.
it_big_tech_vocab_010 Platform capitalism => le capitalisme de plateforme | alts: ['le capitalisme de plateformes']
R Established critical political-economy term; the singular is conventional as a classificatory label, while the plural foregrounds the platform sector.
T It denotes an economic formation organized around data, network effects and gatekeeping, not merely capitalism taking place inside one online platform.
X Le capitalisme de plateforme transfère les risques aux travailleurs et aux fournisseurs, tandis que les intermédiaires contrôlent les données, l’accès et les règles du marché.
E Platform capitalism transfers risks to workers and suppliers while intermediaries control data, access, and market rules.
C ['Le capitalisme de plateforme']
N Old_back is accurate apart from unnecessary capitalization. The alternative records the common plural variant without displacing the conventional singular label.
it_big_tech_vocab_011 the Disruption => la disruption | alts: ['la rupture']
R Established but often promotional anglicism in innovation discourse; critical prose frequently questions whether the promised rupture is real.
T In this domain disruption is not a simple perturbation or service interruption; it claims a radical transformation of a market or institutional model.
X La disruption promise dans l’éducation a surtout privatisé des ressources publiques et rendu les établissements dépendants de plateformes propriétaires difficiles à quitter.
E The disruption promised in education mainly privatized public resources and made institutions dependent on proprietary platforms that are difficult to leave.
C ['La disruption']
N The exact-duplicate report assigns the digital-economy sense to BIG_TECH_VOCAB. Materially corrects old_back, whose perturbation misses the market-transformation claim.
it_big_tech_vocab_012 Digital colonialism => le colonialisme numérique | alts: []
R Critical term in postcolonial studies and technology policy for asymmetric control of infrastructure, standards, data and extracted value.
T It is stronger than dépendance numérique: it frames infrastructural and data control as a structural relationship of domination and extraction.
X Le colonialisme numérique s’enracine lorsque des entreprises étrangères contrôlent les données et les infrastructures, tandis que les sociétés locales participent à peine à la définition des règles.
E Digital colonialism takes root when foreign companies control data and infrastructure while local societies barely participate in defining the rules.
C ['Le colonialisme numérique']
N Old_back is accurate apart from capitalization; the example fixes the structural domination sense rather than treating every foreign technology purchase as colonialism. Self-review restored the definite articles for natural French parallelism.
it_big_tech_vocab_013 the Smart city => la ville intelligente | alts: ['la smart city']
R Ville intelligente is neutral in urban policy; the English loan remains frequent in consulting, municipal promotion and sectoral discourse.
T Smart does not translate as astucieuse here and the term is not limited to connectivity; it covers urban management through sensors, data and automation.
X La ville intelligente perd toute portée démocratique lorsqu’elle achète des capteurs avant de définir des objectifs publics et des limites contraignantes à l’usage des données.
E The smart city loses all democratic substance when it buys sensors before defining public objectives and binding limits on data use.
C ['La ville intelligente']
N Old_back is accurate; the English loan is retained only as a marked sectoral alternative.
it_big_tech_vocab_014 Techno-utopianism => le techno-utopisme | alts: ['l’utopisme technologique']
R Critical term in intellectual history and technology commentary; the analytical alternative is transparent and slightly more formal.
T It is not optimism about one innovation: it attributes to technology the power to lead society toward an ideal order by itself.
X Le techno-utopisme des années quatre-vingt-dix a sous-estimé la rapidité avec laquelle la publicité, la surveillance et les plateformes fermées transformeraient les réseaux ouverts.
E The techno-utopianism of the 1990s underestimated how quickly advertising, surveillance, and closed platforms would transform open networks.
C ['Le techno-utopisme']
N Old_back identifies the correct lexeme but omits the article and unnecessarily capitalizes the common noun. Self-review restored articles in the coordinated subject for idiomatic prose.
it_big_tech_vocab_015 the Datafication => la datafication | alts: ['la mise en données']
R Specialist social-science term; datafication is established in research, while mise en données is a transparent French analytical alternative.
T Datafication is not synonymous with numérisation: it specifically turns activities, relationships or qualities into quantifiable, processable data.
X La datafication de l’école modifie non seulement son administration, mais aussi les critères selon lesquels sont évalués les résultats, les comportements et les besoins d’accompagnement.
E The datafication of schools changes not only their administration but also the criteria by which results, behavior, and support needs are assessed.
C ['La datafication']
N The exact-duplicate report assigns this item to BIG_TECH_VOCAB. Old_back supplies the accepted loan but incorrectly capitalizes the common noun. Self-review restored the articles before the coordinated evaluation targets.
it_big_tech_vocab_016 the Privacy paradox => le paradoxe de la vie privée | alts: []
R Established behavioral-research and data-protection term for the apparent gap between stated privacy concern and observable disclosure behavior.
T The gap between stated concern and observed behavior does not prove indifference: platform design and a lack of alternatives constrain meaningful choice.
X Le paradoxe de la vie privée ne prouve pas que les usagers soient incohérents, puisque les interfaces manipulatrices et l’absence d’alternatives limitent leur capacité réelle de choisir.
E The privacy paradox does not prove that users are inconsistent, since manipulative interfaces and the absence of alternatives limit their real ability to choose.
C ['Le paradoxe de la vie privée']
N Old_back is accurate. Self-review framed the example cautiously so the research label is not mistaken for a causal explanation, improved the final collocation, and removed a narrower confidentiality alternative.
it_big_tech_vocab_019 the Digital detox => la détox numérique | alts: ['la désintoxication numérique']
